From c2e3d55ad935c1a0a2bd6dcc7dfb0f23854929a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 03:35:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] the build shows itself under its own name, not as Kodi

This is a modified build of Kodi that the XBMC Foundation has not
approved. Kodi's trademark policy asks such a build not to use Kodi's
name and logos as its own, and to make clear that it is not supplied by,
endorsed by or a product of the Foundation. So on its own screen and in
its own log the build is "LibreELEC Hades" (utils/ProductName.h).

Texts of the core. The ones that name the program (sixteen, listed in
ProductName.h) stay as Kodi has them, in English and in every language
pack, and the name is exchanged when they are loaded
(KODI::PRODUCT::WithOwnName(), in CLocalizeStrings::Load()). They are
not edited, because a translation counts only while the English text
that it was made from is unchanged: an edited text would be English in
every language. The name is exchanged only where it stands as a word of
its own: next to a letter or a digit it is an inflected form, which is
left (for the characters of two bytes, what is a letter is a table made
from the general categories of Unicode 16.0, so that a no-break space, a
quotation mark of French or the comma of Arabic is none). And not where
a text speaks of Kodi itself: its makers, an address, its trademark.
Two texts keep the name on purpose for that reason (an add-on is "not
compatible with this version of Kodi"). The texts that this project
added say "Hades".

Texts of the skin, whose languages are in the tree: the two that named
the program lose the name in English and in Spanish and have it
exchanged in the other languages (83 texts), with the English text of
every language kept in step. Hebrew, which writes the name in its own
letters, gets the English text for the two; Finnish and French keep the
name where they speak of Kodi's collection of add-ons.

The page of the system information that asked for donations to Kodi is
"About this build": the picture of the build, a text that says what it
is, that it is not supplied by, endorsed by or a product of the XBMC
Foundation, Team Kodi or Team LibreELEC, that they give no support for
it, that Kodi is a registered trademark of the Foundation, and where to
support both projects; and under it the build as the system names it
and the version of Kodi that it is made from. The text has ids of its
own (core 39958 and 39959, skin 31719 and 31720). Where a skin shows
Kodi's thanks to its users in the name of its makers (12397), it gets
that text instead, in English.

The page "Privacy policy" starts with a note of this build: that the
policy under it is Kodi's own, kept as Kodi publishes it; what the build
itself sends (nothing); and what it contacts, without being asked and
when it is used, as Kodi and LibreELEC do.

Also: the entry of the event log at start, the provider of a favourite
that is no add-on's, the name of a timer that is no PVR client's, the
label that a skin may show for the version (the default skin has none),
and one line of the log after "Starting Kodi", which says whose build
this is and asks not to take it to Team Kodi: a log is what gets posted
on their forum.

The images (splash screen, logo, icons) are files of the LibreELEC
recipe, not of this patch.

What other programs read keeps the application's name: paths, the user
agent, JSON-RPC, UPnP and AirPlay identifiers, the add-on interface, the
default name of the device. Those say what the program is compatible
with, and servers and add-ons choose what they do by them.
